A||||13||Necromancer||the evil clutch revisited||||5||
Z||000000||Necromancer||12-10-2000||04:01 PM||||OK. i know there are a great number of threads on clutch problems, but none of them seem to answer my question... so here goes:<P>86 4cyl 5spd... clutch will not disengage. shifts fine with engine off. with slave off bracket, it has planty of travel, no indication of leaks or problems with the master.  when hooked to the tranny, the slave will move the clutch release arm about 1/2" before stopping. placing anything between the slave piston and the arm does not change the distance that the arm moves.<P>when releasing the clutch pedal, the release arm does not push the slave piston very far into the slave.  I can pull the release arm towards the piston another 1/2 at least.<P>I cannot apply enough force to the clutch release lever to move it any farther than the slave does on its own<P>Clutch pedal depresses with very little effort.<P>Near as I can tell, something bad has happened inside the bell housing with the clutch, but I thought I'd confirm that with the group before proceeding.<P>Opinions?<P>Thanks!<P>-Necromancer-<BR>'85 GT<BR>'86 Sport Coupe<P>'86 or '87 GT: Soon as I find a nice one to buy =)<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||2xsess||12-11-2000||07:26 AM||||Need more input, have you bled the system? I had the same problem once. I found that gravity bleeding is the easiest way to do it. I had to bleed mine like twice a day until I got the time to replace the slave cylinder.<BR>Also check Ed Parks website, there's some good info there. <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html</A> <P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by 2xsess (edited 12-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Necromancer||12-11-2000||09:17 AM||||Did the bog standard pedal down, crack bleeder, close bleeder pedal up stuff.  Then opened the bleeder and shoved in the slave piston bit.  <P>For grins and giggles, i put a 1/2 plug i had lying around between the slave piston and the clutch arm (basically taking up the 1/2 the slave will travel).  Pressing the clutch pedal now results in *NO* travel on the clutch arm.  The arm doesn't seem to want to move past the position it is in.<P>Actually, now that i think about it, in the above case, should the pedal be hard to press if the slave isn't moving?  It still goes to the floor with very little effort.<P>I'll try the gravity bleed thing when i get home tonight.  Other than by substitution, is there a way to check proper operation of the slave or master?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||12-11-2000||12:02 PM||||Everything you've reported so far tells me two things: 1)There's slack somewhere in the system between the clutch pedal and the slave cylinder.  2)There may be a hangup of some kind inside the bellhousing.<P>I'm more certain of item #1, than item #2.  You probably knew all this already, but you asked for opinions, and so far that's all I know.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||12-11-2000||02:52 PM||||If you really want to isolate the problem area- unbolt the slave, let the rod extend to its fullest then step on the clutch pedal. If you get any movement then you either have air in the system, slop in the linkage or leakage in the slave or master. A rock hard pedal would indicate problems in the tranny itself||||reg||1
Z||000005||Necromancer||12-11-2000||07:44 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Phil:<BR><B>If you really want to isolate the problem area- unbolt the slave, let the rod extend to its fullest then step on the clutch pedal. {snip} </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hmmm, I hadn't thought of doing that.  I'll take off the slave and see what happens.  Report back later.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||rnoswal||12-11-2000||08:37 PM||||Just another thought. I had the clutch arm crack where the pinch bolt is located. You know, the arm that the clutch slave cylinder rod pushes. We had what sound like the same problem you are having and pulled the tranny, replaced the clutch and then found the bad arm. Oh well at least I now have a new clutch in it! <P>Russ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Necromancer||12-11-2000||08:42 PM||||Took the slave off, pedal still goes to the floor.  Removed slave completely, it was devoid of any fluid... go figure.   disassembled the slave, the bore inside has a ring around the inside about 1/2 way down that looks fairly worn. it looks like fluid has been escaping out the rod end.<P>Replace the slave?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Necromancer||12-12-2000||10:50 PM||||SO I walk into the local parts place today, holding the salve cylinder out of the fiero.  Parts counter guys looks up, looks at the slave and says "Fiero Clutch Slave?" Astounded I say "yeah!". He says "Don't got it, can't get it"<P>Floored me.  GM can order me one for $63 tho, so i'm gonna go that route.||||reg||1
Z||000009||StuGood||12-12-2000||11:34 PM||||With a screen name like yours, would've thought you could pull a new slave cyl. out of your hat, and save the $63  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.  Actually, that somehow doesn't sound like such a bad price, for dealership part.<P>Plus, having a clutch linkage that works well is kind of important...  ||||reg||12
Z||000010||Standard||12-13-2000||05:49 AM||||For any clutch hydraulics, the best place to buy them is the dealership.. that way you won't have trouble with it in a few months.  Heard too many bad things about aftermarket/rebuilt stuff..||||reg||1
Z||000011||Necromancer||12-13-2000||05:54 PM||||Grrrrr.<BR>The GM slave is 'special order' which roughly translates to 'dunno when you will get it'.  I reluctantly purchased a new Raybestos slave w/lifetime warranty (ah the joy of telling the parts counter guy that their computer was wrong...) anyway, I shall be installing it tonight. =)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Motorhead||12-14-2000||03:27 PM||||I agree with Standard on this one!!  After fighting the clutch using aftermarket parts for a year I finally got sick of it and installed GM slave and master cylinders from the dealer.<BR>Master cyl- p/n 10079730  $109.00<BR>Slave cyl - p/n 10035044  $86.50<BR>The dealer didn'thave them in stock, but got them in the next day.<BR>Flushed system with new fluid also.<BR>The clutch feels great, and I'm confident it will be good for another 140,000 miles.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000013||Necromancer||12-18-2000||12:26 AM||||Well, it was definately the slave.  flushed the lines with new brake fluid, installed the new slave, bled, bled and bled some more, fired her up and took it for a drive.... Yayyyyyyyyyyyy!!!!!!<BR>=)<P>Incidentally, the Raybestos slave has a lifetime warranty, so it can go back when it fails next year =P<P>||||reg||1
